      "text": "The given equation represents a circle, and we will rewrite it in the standard form \\((x - h)^2 + (y - k)^2 = r^2\\). To do this, we will complete the square for both the \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) terms.\n\nStarting with the given equation:\n\\[\nx^2 - 6x + y^2 + 2y + 6 = 0\n\\]\n\nFirst, let's complete the square for the \\(x\\) terms:\n\\[\nx^2 - 6x\n\\]\nTo complete the square, we take half of the coefficient of \\(x\\), square it, and add and subtract it inside the equation:\n\\[\nx^2 - 6x = (x - 3)^2 - 9\n\\]\n\nNext, let's complete the square for the \\(y\\) terms:\n\\[\ny^2 + 2y\n\\]\nSimilarly, we take half of the coefficient of \\(y\\), square it, and add and subtract it inside the equation:\n\\[\ny^2 + 2y = (y + 1)^2 - 1\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute these completed squares back into the original equation:\n\\[\n(x - 3)^2 - 9 + (y + 1)^2 - 1 + 6 = 0\n\\]\n\nSimplify the constants on the left-hand side:\n\\[\n(x - 3)^2 - 9 + (y + 1)^2 - 1 + 6 = 0\n\\]\n\\[\n(x - 3)^2 + (y + 1)^2 - 4 = 0\n\\]\n\nAdd 4 to both sides to isolate the completed squares:\n\\[\n(x - 3)^2 + (y + 1)^2 = 4\n\\]\n\nNow, the equation is in the standard form \\((x - h)^2 + (y - k)^2 = r^2\\), where \\((h, k)\\) is the center and \\(r\\) is the radius. From the equation \\((x - 3)^2 + (y + 1)^2 = 4\\), we can see that the center \\((h, k)\\) is \\((3, -1)\\) and the radius \\(r\\) is \\(\\sqrt{4} = 2\\).\n\nThus, the radius of the circle is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
